WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.840
Sami makes lamps in a
small, fictional workshop.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.200
Orders are growing.

3
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.470
But every month, cash feels tight.

4
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.060
The orders became sales in our example.

5
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.470
The problem is timing.

6
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.640
Money leaves the workshop
long before it comes back.

7
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.240
One way to see that timing
is the cash conversion cycle.

8
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.740
BDC, Canada’s business development bank,

9
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.470
describes it as average inventory days,

10
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.799
plus average receivable days,
minus average payable days.

11
00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:32.332
In Sami’s workshop, those
are three clocks on a rail.

12
00:00:32.472 --> 00:00:34.072
The inventory clock.

13
00:00:34.072 --> 00:00:38.732
In our example, materials and finished
lamps wait on the shelves for 30 days,

14
00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:39.861
on average.

15
00:00:40.001 --> 00:00:41.411
The collection clock.

16
00:00:41.411 --> 00:00:45.332
After a sale, customers take
20 days to pay, on average.

17
00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:46.880
The supplier clock.

18
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.996
Sami pays his suppliers 15
days after buying, on average.

19
00:00:51.136 --> 00:00:54.986
30 plus 20, minus 15, is 35 days.

20
00:00:54.986 --> 00:00:58.846
For about 35 days, Sami’s
cash is tied up in the work.

21
00:00:58.986 --> 00:01:02.840
If customers paid 5 days sooner,
with everything else the same,

22
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.036
the cycle would be 30 days.

23
00:01:05.036 --> 00:01:08.196
That’s arithmetic for our
example, not a forecast.

24
00:01:08.336 --> 00:01:10.426
The cycle uses averages.

25
00:01:10.426 --> 00:01:13.206
It isn’t a dated forecast
of your cash balance,

26
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:16.310
and there’s no single right
number for every business.

27
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.726
Supplier payments should still
follow the terms you agreed.

28
00:01:19.866 --> 00:01:23.786
So map the timing before assuming
more sales will fix your cash.

29
00:01:23.786 --> 00:01:27.867
Check three clocks: inventory,
collection, and suppliers.

30
00:01:28.007 --> 00:01:31.020
In this credit-sale example,
the sale comes first.

31
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.639
Cash arrives later.

32
00:01:32.779 --> 00:01:34.249
Ahmadreza Samadi.

33
00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:36.186
